{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '247,318p;390,525p;590,706p'\"","aggregated_output":"   247\t### 4.1 The format record\n   248\t\n   249\tA format record is a plain map held in linker-local state. B6 fixed three\n   250\tfunction slots; this revision replaces one, renames one, and adds four:\n   251\t\n   252\t```clojure\n   253\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code      ; the dispatch key\n   254\t :contract            \"b1\"                    ; contract revision name\n   255\t :identity-fn         (fn [value] identity)   ; mint: publish! only\n   256\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [identity value] boolean) ; step 3\n   257\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 2, per part\n   258\t :validate-fn         (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 4, whole\n   259\t :obligations-fn      (fn [value] records)           ; step 5a, below\n   260\t :definitions-fn      (fn [value] records)           ; step 5a, below\n   261\t :applications-fn     (fn [value] records)           ; step 5a, below\n   262\t :parts-fn            (fn [value] [address ...])}   ; step 2 worklist\n   263\t```\n   264\t\n   265\t- `:identity-fn` mints an identity from a payload. It is used by\n   266\t  `publish!` and never by verification.\n   267\t- `:identity-matches-fn` is the step-3 check, directed by the identity.\n   268\t  For H and R it is `(= identity (image-hash value))`, a contract-pinned\n   269\t  hash with one algorithm. For the storage-derived formats it is\n   270\t  `(jing/segment-matches? identity value)`, so a value minted under\n   271\t  `:sha256` while the default is `:blake3` verifies under the algorithm\n   272\t  the identity names and never fails spuriously.\n   273\t- `:row-defect-fn` validates one fetched part on its own, before any\n   274\t  address it references is enqueued: tag, arity, slot kinds, saturation.\n   275\t  For single-payload formats it is the whole validator, run early.\n   276\t- `:parts-fn` returns the storage addresses a fetched payload references\n   277\t  and without which it is incomplete. For the three single-payload\n   278\t  formats it returns nothing. For `:yin.ast/code` it returns the child\n   279\t  row ids of a row body.\n   280\t- `:obligations-fn`, `:definitions-fn`, and `:applications-fn` are\n   281\t  **position-bearing scanners** with one shared result shape:\n   282\t\n   283\t  ```clojure\n   284\t  ;; :obligations-fn  value -> [{:name sym :at position :in-body? b}]\n   285\t  ;; :definitions-fn  value -> [{:name sym :at position\n   286\t  ;;                             :conditional? b}]\n   287\t  ;; :applications-fn value -> [{:at position}]\n   288\t  ;; position: a tree occurrence path [root-address path] for\n   289\t  ;;           :yin.ast/code; a pc for the vector formats\n   290\t  ```\n   291\t\n   292\t  `:conditional?` is true when any `:if` branch, jump target range, or\n   293\t  lambda body encloses the definition; `:in-body?` is true when a\n   294\t  lambda body encloses the occurrence; an `:applications-fn` record is\n   295\t  one **application site**: a position at which this image may apply a\n   296\t  closure it creates -- a direct call site, a call of an imported\n   297\t  name, or a call of a primitive that receives a function argument,\n   298\t  because the callee may apply what it is handed. Dominance between\n   299\t  two positions is decided by the linker from these fields and the\n   300\t  format's order (step 5a). For the AST record all three scanners are\n   301\t  Datalog over the rows: `yin.vm/free-names` today returns a symbol\n   302\t  set, and is extended (or wrapped by an occurrence-scoped query over\n   303\t  the same rules) to return each occurrence's path; the definitions\n   304\t  scanner is the store-key query behind `yin.vm/ast-requirements`\n   305\t  with the binding row's path and its enclosing tags; the application\n   306\t  scanner is the application-row query over the same relation. For\n   307\t  the vector formats all three are operand scans by pc (`:var`\n   308\t  against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the `yin/def` call\n   309\t  sites, the call opcodes for applications) with conditional target\n   310\t  ranges taken from the `:jump-if` and body operands. A format whose\n   311\t  scanner returns positions as `nil` is admitted and falls under the\n   312\t  conservative degradation of step 5a: every occurrence is retained\n   313\t  as an obligation, and with no application positions no occurrence\n   314\t  inside a lambda body is discharged at all.\n   315\t\n   316\t`fetch` and `step` contain no branch on `:format`. Adding a fifth backend\n   317\tis one more record.\n   318\t\n   390\t   format, the map of every fetched part by address.\n   391\t3. **Verify format identity.** `(:identity-matches-fn format)` over the\n   392\t   requested identity and the root value. For H and R this is a\n   393\t   different hash under a different preimage from step 2 and catches a\n   394\t   stale or swapped index entry, a descriptor disagreement, or a\n   395\t   contract version disagreement (all `:hash-mismatch`, as in B6). For\n   396\t   the storage-derived formats it is `segment-matches?` under the\n   397\t   identity's own algorithm; it still runs, because it is the only check\n   398\t   that the *index* did not point the identity at a different, correctly\n   399\t   stored payload.\n   400\t4. **Validate structural and liveness invariants.** The format's\n   401\t   validator runs over the complete value: for a tree, over the\n   402\t   assembled row set (`:id-resolves`, `:acyclic`, `:root-reachable` are\n   403\t   whole-tree rules that step 2's row-local check cannot run); for a\n   404\t   vector, over the vector; for a register image, including the\n   405\t   ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A validator that throws is\n   406\t   `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:validator-refused`.\n   407\t5. **Verify dependency closure.** Two halves.\n   408\t   - *5a, at the linker: obligations.* Two scans run over the verified\n   409\t     value. `:obligations-fn` yields the free-name occurrences, each\n   410\t     with its position: for a tree, the occurrence path and whether it\n   411\t     lies inside a `:lambda` body; for a vector, the pc and whether it\n   412\t     lies in a body rather than the main sequence. `:definitions-fn`\n   413\t     yields the **definition set** with positions: every constant store\n   414\t     key the image binds at module level (the section 7.7 store-key\n   415\t     query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`: `:vm/store-put` rows,\n   416\t     `:store-put` operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key),\n   417\t     each with the position of the binding in the main sequence.\n   418\t     A definition discharges an occurrence by **defined-before-use\n   419\t     under control-flow dominance**, not by subtraction of names and\n   420\t     not by source order alone. Source order says a definition is\n   421\t     written earlier; dominance says it has executed by the time the\n   422\t     occurrence runs. Only the second discharges, and where dominance\n   423\t     cannot be established the obligation is **retained**, never\n   424\t     dropped:\n   425\t     - A definition is *unconditional* when it sits in the main\n   426\t       sequence at top level or inside a `:do` (for a tree) or at a pc\n   427\t       in the main sequence outside every conditional target range\n   428\t       (for a vector); it is *conditional* when any `:if` branch, any\n   429\t       jump target, or any lambda body encloses it. A conditional\n   430\t       definition discharges nothing statically: the linker cannot know\n   431\t       the branch was taken, so an occurrence that depends on it keeps\n   432\t       its obligation and is checked at the receiver under 5b exactly\n   433\t       as an undefined name would be, and the manifest must declare it\n   434\t       or the link is `:undeclared-free`. This is conservative by\n   435\t       design: a module that defines a name only in one branch and\n   436\t       reads it afterwards must declare what the name means when the\n   437\t       branch is not taken.\n   438\t     - An occurrence in the main sequence (outside every lambda body)\n   439\t       is discharged only by an unconditional definition of that name\n   440\t       that dominates it: for a tree, an earlier sibling in the same\n   441\t       or an enclosing top-level `:do`; for a vector, an earlier pc in\n   442\t       the main sequence with no conditional target range between the\n   443\t       two. A main-sequence occurrence whose only definitions are all\n   444\t       later or all conditional and later is `:use-before-definition`:\n   445\t       in the isolated store it would read nothing, and no manifest\n   446\t       declaration may stand in for a name the module itself defines\n   447\t       unconditionally later. An occurrence whose only definitions are\n   448\t       conditional and earlier retains its obligation, per the rule\n   449\t       above.\n   450\t     - An occurrence inside a lambda body is discharged only by an\n   451\t       unconditional definition of that name that is **proven to\n   452\t       execute before every application of the enclosing closure**.\n   453\t       The body runs at application time, and the applications the\n   454\t       image performs are exactly its application sites\n   455\t       (`:applications-fn`, section 4.1). No value-flow analysis\n   456\t       bounds which closures a site can apply, so every site is\n   457\t       treated as a possible application of every closure the image\n   458\t       creates: a definition discharges a body occurrence only when\n   459\t       it dominates every application site in the main sequence,\n   460\t       under the same order rules as a main-sequence occurrence. A\n   461\t       site inside another body needs no separate case: it executes\n   462\t       only after some main-sequence site has executed, which the\n   463\t       definition dominates, or after halt, by which an unconditional\n   464\t       definition has executed. An image with no application site\n   465\t       discharges a body occurrence against every unconditional\n   466\t       definition of the name: nothing applies the closure before\n   467\t       halt, and after halt the read runs against the module store\n   468\t       the relocation carries (section 7.3), never the receiver's\n   469\t       ambient bindings. Where any site is not dominated, the\n   470\t       obligation is **retained** and must be declared by the\n   471\t       manifest exactly as a conditional definition's must. Revision\n   472\t       r5 discharged a body occurrence against any later\n   473\t       unconditional definition and left an early application to\n   474\t       \"fail in the child at run time\"; the second half was wrong:\n   475\t       the read does not reliably error, because `resolve-var`\n   476\t       (`yin.vm.engine`) falls through to primitives and the module\n   477\t       registry and may bind an ambient name whose profile was never\n   478\t       checked. r6 withdraws the claim and retains the obligation.\n   479\t     Where a scanner cannot compute positions at all, the rule\n   480\t     degrades to the conservative side: every occurrence keeps its\n   481\t     obligation and no definition discharges anything. Under that\n   482\t     degradation a self-defining module still links only if its\n   483\t     manifest declares every name it reads, which is more obligation\n   484\t     than necessary and never less.\n   485\t     Each undischarged occurrence is joined with the manifest that\n   486\t     delivered the image (section 8.1): a name under\n   487\t     `:yin.module/primitives` becomes\n   488\t     `{:name n :kind :primitive :profile addr}`; a name exported by a\n   489\t     module under `:yin.module/requires` becomes\n   490\t     `{:name n :kind :module :module m :manifest addr}`. A free name\n   491\t     the manifest declares nowhere is `:undeclared-free`; the publisher\n   492\t     did not say what the name means, so no receiver can bind it\n   493\t     identically. The definition set is **advisory for exports**: an\n   494\t     export under `:yin.module/exports` that the static scan finds is\n   495\t     known to be bound if the body halts, and one it does not find\n   496\t     (a key computed at run time) is neither refused nor assumed. Every\n   497\t     export is verified at run time in the `validated` phase of section\n   498\t     7.3, which is the only export check; revision r3's early static\n   499\t     `:export-missing` is withdrawn. An image requested by bare identity\n   500\t     with no manifest has no declarations; it verifies only if every\n   501\t     free occurrence is discharged by its own definitions, which is B6's\n   502\t     closed-image case and remains supported.\n   503\t   - *5b, at the receiver: discharge.* Obligations travel with the\n   504\t     verified image in the response. The receiver checks each against\n   505\t     its live state as the first act of installation, before any load:\n   506\t     a `:primitive` obligation is discharged only by a primitive of\n   507\t     equal profile address; a `:module` obligation only by a linked\n   508\t     module of equal manifest address; a name bound in the receiver's\n   509\t     free env or store is `:shadowed-free`; anything else is\n   510\t     `:unresolved-free` naming the obligation and what was found. This\n   511\t     is UCF section 7.5.2's rule (\"checked by profile, not by presence\")\n   512\t     at the linker's grain, and D11's refusal for an image the receiver\n   513\t     cannot bind identically.\n   514\t   Discharge is at the receiver because the receiver's store and free\n   515\t   env are live state; a snapshot sent on the wire is stale by\n   516\t   construction and would license a binding that no longer holds. The\n   517\t   host-policy `fetch` (section 6.4), which runs in one process with the\n   518\t   receiver in hand, runs both halves before returning.\n   519\t6. **Return the verified image.** `{:status :ok :format f :identity i\n   520\t   :address a :value v :obligations [...]}` plus `:parts {address value}`\n   521\t   for a multi-part format and `:derivation {...}` where a manifest\n   522\t   supplied one (section 8.1). Nothing partially verified is ever\n   523\t   returned.\n   524\t\n   525\t### 4.3 Refusal vocabulary\n   590\tThree records bind existing code; the AST record's free-name scanner is\n   591\tthe section 4.5 query of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, implemented as\n   592\t`yin.vm/free-names`.\n   593\t\n   594\t### 5.1 `:yin.ast/code` (the AST walker)\n   595\t\n   596\t```clojure\n   597\t{:format              :yin.ast/code\n   598\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   599\t :identity-fn         (fn [body] (jing/segment-key body))\n   600\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [root-id body] (jing/segment-matches? root-id\n   601\t                                                                  body))\n   602\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [body] (row-local-defect body))\n   603\t :validate-fn         (fn [tree] (yin.vm/validate-rows tree))\n   604\t :obligations-fn      (fn [tree] (free-name-occurrences tree))\n   605\t :definitions-fn      (fn [tree] (definition-occurrences tree))\n   606\t :applications-fn     (fn [tree] (application-sites tree))\n   607\t :parts-fn            (fn [body] (body-child-ids body))}\n   608\t```\n   609\t\n   610\tPayload: one row body `[tag & slots]` per address, exactly as\n   611\t`yin.vm.content/materialize-tree!` stores it (D3: individual rows, no pack\n   612\tformat; the body is what is hashed, and the id is derived from it). The\n   613\tidentity is the root row's id. Steps 2 and 3 therefore hash the fetched\n   614\tbody as received; the address is prepended only when the linker assembles\n   615\t`{id [id tag & slots]}` for step 4, the shape `validate-rows` takes.\n   616\t`row-local-defect` runs the `:tag`, `:arity`, `:slot-kind`, and\n   617\t`:saturation` rules of section 7.4 over one body, so a malformed row\n   618\tnever has its slots followed. Step 2's worklist follows `node` and\n   619\t`nodes` slots by the grammar's positions, the walk\n   620\t`yin.vm.content/load-rows` performs today; that function's throwing\n   621\tsurface is retired in favor of the linker's refusals (section 9).\n   622\tAll three scanners return the position-bearing records of section\n   623\t4.1: `free-name-occurrences` is `yin.vm/free-names` wrapped to carry\n   624\teach occurrence's path and `:in-body?`; `definition-occurrences` is\n   625\tthe store-key query carrying each binding row's path and\n   626\t`:conditional?`; `application-sites` is the application-row query.\n   627\tLoading is `yin.vm.ast-walker/vm-load-rows`.\n   628\t\n   629\t### 5.2 `:yin.semantic/code` (the semantic VM)\n   630\t\n   631\t```clojure\n   632\t{:format              :yin.semantic/code\n   633\t :contract            \"v2\"\n   634\t :identity-fn         jing/segment-key\n   635\t :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?\n   636\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   637\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.code/well-formed-vector?\n   638\t :obligations-fn      semantic-free-names\n   639\t :definitions-fn      semantic-definitions\n   640\t :applications-fn     semantic-application-sites\n   641\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   642\t```\n   643\t\n   644\tPayload: the canonical positional instruction tuple vector (UCF section\n   645\t7.3.2), stored as the exact vector at its own `segment-key`; nothing\n   646\telse may hash there (UCF section 7.3.4). The identity is that address.\n   647\t`well-formed-vector?` is the section 7.5 grammar. `semantic-free-names`\n   648\tscans `:var` operands not bound by an enclosing closure's params, the\n   649\t`$code` query of section 7.7; `semantic-application-sites` scans the\n   650\tcall opcodes, including calls of imported names and of primitives\n   651\tthat receive a function argument; all three return the section 4.1\n   652\trecords. The linker module defines them, as B6 defined the two de\n   653\tBruijn scanners. The contract stamp is UCF's\n   654\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v2\"}`; a link request for this format carries the\n   655\tstamp, and the receiver's index is keyed per stamp (UCF section 7.3.3).\n   656\tLoading is `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`, which writes the address to the\n   657\timage and the alias column.\n   658\t\n   659\t### 5.3 `:yin.debruijn.code` (the stack VM)\n   660\t\n   661\t```clojure\n   662\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code\n   663\t :contract            \"b1\"\n   664\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   665\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [H v] (= H (yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash v)))\n   666\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   667\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   668\t :obligations-fn      stack-free-names\n   669\t :definitions-fn      stack-definitions\n   670\t :applications-fn     stack-application-sites\n   671\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   672\t```\n   673\t\n   674\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.1 in substance. The identity H is\n   675\t`image-hash` over the descriptor hash (which embeds the lowering contract\n   676\tversion) and the canonical vector; the `=` here compares two values of\n   677\tone contract-pinned hash and is not a Class 3 site. Free names are\n   678\t`:load-free` operands; application sites are `:call` operands,\n   679\tincluding calls of imported names and function-argument primitives.\n   680\tLoading is `yin.vm.debruijn.stack/load-image`;\n   681\t`yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` shows the relocation a second image\n   682\tneeds beside a held one.\n   683\t\n   684\t### 5.4 `:yin.debruijn.register` (the register VM)\n   685\t\n   686\t```clojure\n   687\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.register\n   688\t :contract            \"r1\"\n   689\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   690\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [R v]\n   691\t                        (= R (yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   692\t                               v)))\n   693\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   694\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   695\t :obligations-fn      register-free-names\n   696\t :definitions-fn      register-definitions\n   697\t :applications-fn     register-application-sites\n   698\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   699\t```\n   700\t\n   701\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.2 in substance. Validation includes the\n   702\tlive-set rules; free names are `[:load-free rd name]` operands across all\n   703\tbodies; application sites are the call operands. Loading is\n   704\t`yin.vm.debruijn.register/load-image`.\n   705\t\n   706\t### 5.5 Same-root pairing becomes derivation verification\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
