{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1190,1334p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1190\t  **The closure marker, and its lift and lower per binding discipline.**\n  1191\t  UCF section 7.5.1 defines the closure marker for the named semantic\n  1192\t  VM. The de Bruijn kernels are positional and nameless, so the marker\n  1193\t  gains a `:yin.k/binding` key with two variants, and a module's\n  1194\t  closures carry the `:yin.k/store-of` key of the module store; both\n  1195\t  are amendments to UCF listed in section 11, item 12:\n  1196\t\n  1197\t  ```clojure\n  1198\t  ;; named (:yin.ast/code, :yin.semantic/code)\n  1199\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :named\n  1200\t   :yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc\n  1201\t   :yin.k/params [sym ...] :yin.k/env {sym encoded}\n  1202\t   :yin.k/store-of m}                       ; when module-defined\n  1203\t\n  1204\t  ;; positional (:yin.debruijn.code, :yin.debruijn.register)\n  1205\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :positional\n  1206\t   :yin.k/segment identity :yin.k/entry pc\n  1207\t   :yin.k/arity n :yin.k/frames [[encoded ...] ...]\n  1208\t   :yin.k/store-of m}                       ; when module-defined\n  1209\t  ```\n  1210\t\n  1211\t  | Kernel closure                       | Lift                          |\n  1212\t  |--------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n  1213\t  | semantic `{:type :closure :params p  | `addr` = alias column inverse |\n  1214\t  | :entry e :segment local :env E}`     | of `local`; `pc` = `e`;       |\n  1215\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1216\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1217\t  | walker `{:type :closure :params p    | `addr` = the row id of the    |\n  1218\t  | :body node :env E}` (the body is an  | `:lambda` row whose body slot |\n  1219\t  | AST node, not a code coordinate)     | is `node`, found by the walk  |\n  1220\t  |                                      | below; `pc` = `nil`;          |\n  1221\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1222\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1223\t  | stack / register `{:type :closure    | offset-table entry with       |\n  1224\t  | :arity n :body-pc p :frames F}`      | `off <= p < off + len` gives  |\n  1225\t  |                                      | `identity` and `pc = p - off`;|\n  1226\t  |                                      | `arity` = `n`; `frames` =     |\n  1227\t  |                                      | `mapv (mapv encode) F`,       |\n  1228\t  |                                      | outermost frame first         |\n  1229\t\n  1230\t  | Marker variant | Lower into the parent                              |\n  1231\t  |----------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n  1232\t  | `:named`,      | `local'` = parent alias column at `addr`; `:entry` |\n  1233\t  | semantic       | unchanged; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =           |\n  1234\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1235\t  | `:named`,      | `:body` = the body slot of the parent's row at     |\n  1236\t  | walker         | `addr`, reconstructed as a node by the walker's    |\n  1237\t  |                | row decoder; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =         |\n  1238\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1239\t  | `:positional`  | `off'` = parent offset-table entry for `identity`; |\n  1240\t  |                | `:body-pc` = `off' + pc`; `:arity` = `n`;          |\n  1241\t  |                | `:frames` = `mapv (mapv decode) frames`            |\n  1242\t\n  1243\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\n  1244\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\n  1245\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of exactly one\n  1246\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\n  1247\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The lift\n  1248\t  finds it by the walker's own row index (`node -> row id`, which\n  1249\t  `vm-load-rows` builds when it decodes rows into nodes and which\n  1250\t  `attach-image` extends), and refuses the lift as\n  1251\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :unrooted-body` when the node\n  1252\t  is in no held row, which can happen only for a closure a host\n  1253\t  function fabricated. The origin image of a walker closure is the\n  1254\t  tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda` row id is\n  1255\t  content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two trees is one id\n  1256\t  and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads the parent's row at\n  1257\t  that id (present after act 3) and decodes its body slot into a node\n  1258\t  through the same decoder the loader uses, so the closure's `:body`\n  1259\t  in the parent is structurally equal to the child's and its `:params`\n  1260\t  and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\n  1261\t\n  1262\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\n  1263\t  marker only into a positional kernel of the same `:format`; the\n  1264\t  reverse is `:binding-mismatch`. This never arises within one install,\n  1265\t  because the child is a VM of the same backend as the parent (section\n  1266\t  7.3, first rule); it is stated so that a lifted slice carried\n  1267\t  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\n  1268\t  closure between disciplines is a re-lowering of source under a\n  1269\t  derivation record (section 8.1), never a relocation. `decode` and\n  1270\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\n  1271\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\n  1272\t  origin set of act 2.\n  1273\t\n  1274\t  **The module store (r6).** A child runs with an isolated store, and\n  1275\t  its exports must not leak that isolation. A module that binds\n  1276\t  `(def x 1)` and exports `(fn [] x)` publishes a closure whose free\n  1277\t  read of `x` would, if only the closure value crossed the boundary,\n  1278\t  resolve through the receiving task's store and fall through to its\n  1279\t  primitives and module registry -- where `x` is unbound or bound to\n  1280\t  something the publisher never saw. The store therefore crosses with\n  1281\t  the exports, under UCF section 7.6.2's slice discipline:\n  1282\t  - *Lift.* The child's halted store is encoded beside the exports\n  1283\t    as `:yin.k/store`, by UCF's value grammar; whatever that grammar\n  1284\t    refuses (a host handle the body parked on) refuses the lift and\n  1285\t    the install.\n  1286\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\n  1287\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\n  1288\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\n  1289\t    11, item 12. The store travels once per slice, not per closure.\n  1290\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task decodes the slice into\n  1291\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state. Nothing is\n  1292\t    written into any task's ambient store: this is 7.6.2's \"there is\n  1293\t    no merge\" rule at the module grain -- a receiver binding of the\n  1294\t    same name can neither supply nor shadow the module's value.\n  1295\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, its\n  1296\t    free reads resolve through the module store of `m` first and the\n  1297\t    receiver's free env, primitives, and registry second; the ambient\n  1298\t    store does not participate. A store write from inside such a body\n  1299\t    routes to the module store. Sibling exports in one task share\n  1300\t    one store instance, so a write one makes is visible to the next;\n  1301\t    each task holds its own instance, so writes never cross tasks. A\n  1302\t    declared obligation the store does not hold falls through to the\n  1303\t    receiver's bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a\n  1304\t    conditional definition that ran overwrites the module's own\n  1305\t    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\n  1306\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: an\n  1307\t    export a child re-exports from a dependency carries that\n  1308\t    dependency's `:store-of` and reads its store, not the child's\n  1309\t    ambient bindings.\n  1310\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1311\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1312\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1313\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1314\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1315\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\n  1316\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1317\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1318\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1319\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1320\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1321\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1322\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n  1323\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n  1324\t  again.\n  1325\t\n  1326\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n  1327\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n  1328\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n  1329\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n  1330\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n  1331\tgrain.\n  1332\t\n  1333\t### 7.4 Transitive requires and cycles\n  1334\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
