{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '812,852p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1478,1555p'\"","aggregated_output":"   812\t### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\n   813\t\n   814\tThe store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI\n   815\tpair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A\n   816\tcontinuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed\n   817\tpoint of §7.6.1 can name — every free `:var` obligation for which the\n   818\temitter's store has a matching key, every `:store-get`/`:store-put` operand,\n   819\tevery stream/cursor cell — with values encoded by §7.5:\n   820\t\n   821\t```clojure\n   822\t:yin.k/store {counter 41\n   823\t              'log {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}}\n   824\t```\n   825\t\n   826\tThe FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store\n   827\tkeys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has\n   828\tits own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.\n   829\t\n   830\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n   831\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n   832\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n   833\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n   834\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n   835\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n   836\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n   837\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n   838\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n   839\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n   840\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n   841\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n   842\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n   843\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n   844\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n   845\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n   846\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n   847\taccident.\n   848\t\n   849\t### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\n   850\t\n   851\tThe reference machine's scheduler state does not live in the store, and the\n   852\tfirst draft transported none of it. Both omissions are defects: the\n  1478\tNames live outside the manifest. A name environment maps a module name\n  1479\tto a manifest address and is linker-local state in one of two shapes:\n  1480\t\n  1481\t- a plain map `{'my.lib :segment/...}`, for tests and single-process\n  1482\t  compositions, with the composition itself as the sole asserter;\n  1483\t- assertions read from a `dao.space` source under the policy below.\n  1484\t\n  1485\t**Fail-closed assertion policy.** This policy is an entry criterion for\n  1486\tM4: no linker may resolve a name from `dao.space` assertions before it is\n  1487\timplemented and tested.\n  1488\t\n  1489\t- **Every authority event is a signed envelope.** An assertion and a\n  1490\t  retraction are two distinct envelope shapes, each a plain map whose\n  1491\t  content address (`dao.jing/segment-key`) is the event's id:\n  1492\t\n  1493\t  ```clojure\n  1494\t  ;; assertion\n  1495\t  {:yin.module/op          :assert\n  1496\t   :yin.module/name        'my.lib\n  1497\t   :yin.module/manifest    :segment/...\n  1498\t   :yin.module/asserted-by principal\n  1499\t   :yin.module/seq         41}          ; per-principal, strictly rising\n  1500\t\n  1501\t  ;; retraction: bound to one assertion by that assertion's id\n  1502\t  {:yin.module/op          :retract\n  1503\t   :yin.module/of          :segment/... ; segment-key of the assertion\n  1504\t   :yin.module/asserted-by principal\n  1505\t   :yin.module/seq         42}\n  1506\t  ```\n  1507\t\n  1508\t  The envelope is transacted as datoms `[ev :yin.module/envelope env]`\n  1509\t  and `[ev :yin.module/proof proof]`. An envelope is honored only with\n  1510\t  a proof; a bare `:asserted-by` value is a string anyone can write.\n  1511\t  An envelope whose proof is absent or fails is `:unauthenticated`\n  1512\t  with `:kind :no-proof` or `:bad-proof` and is discarded before\n  1513\t  counting. A retraction whose `:yin.module/of` names no assertion at\n  1514\t  the snapshot, or an assertion by a different principal, is discarded\n  1515\t  with `:kind :dangling-retraction`; a retraction retracts exactly the\n  1516\t  assertion it names and nothing by name alone.\n  1517\t- **Replay is prevented by the per-principal sequence.** Every envelope\n  1518\t  a principal issues carries `:yin.module/seq`, strictly increasing\n  1519\t  over that principal's lifetime; the composition declares each\n  1520\t  principal's floor in `:authority` (`0` for a new principal, the\n  1521\t  highest sequence already honored for a re-declared one). At the\n  1522\t  snapshot the linker processes one principal's proven envelopes in\n  1523\t  three passes, in this order:\n  1524\t  1. *Deduplicate by content id.* Every envelope is content; two\n  1525\t     appearances of one envelope have one `segment-key` and are one\n  1526\t     event. All but the first appearance are dropped silently, as\n  1527\t     `dao.jing` drops a re-materialization: an exact duplicate is a\n  1528\t     harmless carrier replay (a forwarder, a retry after `full`, a\n  1529\t     mirrored log) and is neither a defect nor evidence against the\n  1530\t     principal. This pass runs first so that repeating a valid envelope\n  1531\t     can never suppress it.\n  1532\t  2. *Detect equivocation.* After deduplication, two *distinct*\n  1533\t     envelopes from one principal with equal sequence are equivocation:\n  1534\t     the principal signed two different claims under one sequence\n  1535\t     number. Both are discarded with `:kind :equivocation`, and the\n  1536\t     principal's later envelopes at the snapshot are discarded too. A\n  1537\t     duplicate can no longer reach this pass, so equivocation is\n  1538\t     attributable to the signer alone.\n  1539\t  3. *Order and honor.* The remaining envelopes are ordered by sequence\n  1540\t     and each is honored only if its sequence exceeds the floor and\n  1541\t     every sequence honored before it; an envelope at or below either\n  1542\t     is a replay of an old claim under a fresh carrier and is discarded\n  1543\t     with `:kind :replay`.\n  1544\t  A monotone sequence is chosen over nonces because it makes the check\n  1545\t  stateless over the snapshot: no nonce store is needed and no window\n  1546\t  is guessed.\n  1547\t  A wall-clock timestamp is admitted as an optional\n  1548\t  `:yin.module/issued-at` in the envelope for audit, and is never used\n  1549\t  to order or to accept.\n  1550\t- Two proof kinds are admitted, and the composition's `:authority`\n  1551\t  declares, per principal, which one it expects:\n  1552\t  - *Signature.* `proof` is `{:yin.module/signature sig}`, where `sig`\n  1553\t    signs the canonical bytes (`dao.jing/canonical-bytes`) of the whole\n  1554\t    envelope under the principal's public key, so operation type, name,\n  1555\t    manifest, principal, and sequence are all covered and none can be\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc | sed -n '430,486p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc | sed -n '420,482p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | sed -n '195,242p'\"","aggregated_output":"   430\t                                (conj continuation\n   431\t                                      {:return-pc (inc pc), :frames frames,\n   432\t                                       :stack-base (count stack')}))]\n   433\t            (assoc vm\n   434\t                   :pc (:body-pc f)\n   435\t                   :frames body-frames\n   436\t                   :stack stack'\n   437\t                   :continuation continuation'))\n   438\t\n   439\t          ;; A primitive host function, resolved by :load-free. The same\n   440\t          ;; path the named engine's `apply-call` uses for a `fn?` callee:\n   441\t          ;; a plain result lands on the stack; an effect descriptor (a\n   442\t          ;; `yin/def`, a `require`, a `stream` module call) is dispatched\n   443\t          ;; through the engine and may park with the continuation after\n   444\t          ;; the call site.\n   445\t          (fn? f)\n   446\t          (let [result (apply f args)]\n   447\t            (if (module/effect? result)\n   448\t              (run-effect vm result stack')\n   449\t              (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack' result))))\n   450\t\n   451\t          :else\n   452\t          (throw (ex-info \"Cannot apply non-function\" {:fn f}))))\n   453\t\n   454\t      :return\n   455\t      (let [val (peek stack)\n   456\t            frame (peek continuation)]\n   457\t        (if frame\n   458\t          (assoc vm\n   459\t                 :pc (:return-pc frame)\n   460\t                 :frames (:frames frame)\n   461\t                 :stack (conj (subvec stack 0 (:stack-base frame)) val)\n   462\t                 :continuation (pop continuation))\n   463\t          (assoc vm :halted? true, :stack [val], :value val)))\n   464\t\n   465\t      :jump\n   466\t      (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1))\n   467\t\n   468\t      :branch-false\n   469\t      (let [val (peek stack), stack' (pop stack)]\n   470\t        (if val\n   471\t          (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack stack')\n   472\t          (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1) :stack stack')))\n   473\t\n   474\t      :halt\n   475\t      (assoc vm :halted? true, :value (peek stack))\n   476\t\n   477\t      :store-get\n   478\t      (let [key (nth inst 1)]\n   479\t        (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack (get store key))))\n   480\t\n   481\t      :store-put\n   482\t      (let [key (nth inst 1), value (nth inst 2)]\n   483\t        (assoc vm\n   484\t               :pc (inc pc)\n   485\t               :stack (conj stack value)\n   486\t               :store (assoc store key value)))\n   420\t        (cond\n   421\t          (and (map? f) (= :closure (:type f)))\n   422\t          (let [callee-pc (:body-pc f)\n   423\t                callee-body (body-of-pc segment callee-pc)\n   424\t                callee-arity (:arity f)\n   425\t                callee-reg-count (:registers callee-body)\n   426\t                locals (bind-positional callee-arity args)\n   427\t                body-frames (conj (:frames f) locals)\n   428\t                callee-regs (into locals\n   429\t                                  (repeat (- callee-reg-count callee-arity)\n   430\t                                          nil))\n   431\t                continuation'\n   432\t                (if tail?\n   433\t                  continuation\n   434\t                  (conj continuation\n   435\t                        {:segment segment,\n   436\t                         :hash hash,\n   437\t                         :site-pc pc,\n   438\t                         :return-pc (inc pc),\n   439\t                         :frames frames,\n   440\t                         :regs (mapv (fn [r] [r (nth registers r)]) live),\n   441\t                         :live live,\n   442\t                         :dest rd}))]\n   443\t            (assoc vm\n   444\t                   :pc callee-pc\n   445\t                   :frames body-frames\n   446\t                   :registers callee-regs\n   447\t                   :continuation continuation'))\n   448\t\n   449\t          (fn? f)\n   450\t          (let [result (apply f args)]\n   451\t            (if (module/effect? result)\n   452\t              (run-call-effect vm result inst)\n   453\t              (if tail?\n   454\t                (return-transition vm result)\n   455\t                (assoc vm\n   456\t                       :pc (inc pc)\n   457\t                       :registers (assoc registers rd result)))))\n   458\t\n   459\t          :else\n   460\t          (throw (ex-info \"Cannot apply non-function\" {:fn f}))))\n   461\t\n   462\t      :branch-false\n   463\t      (let [c (nth registers (nth inst 1))]\n   464\t        (assoc vm :pc (if c (inc pc) (nth inst 2))))\n   465\t\n   466\t      :jump\n   467\t      (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1))\n   468\t\n   469\t      :return\n   470\t      (return-transition vm (nth registers (nth inst 1)))\n   471\t\n   472\t      :halt\n   473\t      (assoc vm :halted? true, :value (nth registers (nth inst 1)))\n   474\t\n   475\t      :store-get\n   476\t      (assoc vm\n   477\t             :pc (inc pc)\n   478\t             :registers (assoc registers (nth inst 1) (get store (nth inst 2))))\n   479\t\n   480\t      :store-put\n   481\t      (let [key (nth inst 2)\n   482\t            val (nth inst 3)]\n   195\t(defn- apply-call\n   196\t  \"The §4.2 call transition, with the arguments already isolated from the\n   197\t   operand stack. Returns `{:goto [seg pc val St E K vm image]}` to continue,\n   198\t   `{:stop vm}` when the call blocked, or throws for a non-function.\n   199\t\n   200\t   A tail call grows neither K nor St: the frame the caller would have pushed\n   201\t   is the frame its own caller gave it. A primitive that yields an effect\n   202\t   parks with the continuation after the call site.\"\n   203\t  [code seg pc val St E K vm tail? f args]\n   204\t  (cond\n   205\t    (= :closure (:type f))\n   206\t    (let [E' (merge (:env f) (engine/bind-params (:params f) args))\n   207\t          frame {:type :return, :segment seg, :pc (inc pc), :env E,\n   208\t                 :stack-base (count St)}\n   209\t          seg' (:segment f)]\n   210\t      {:goto [seg' (:entry f) val St E' (if tail? K (conj K frame))\n   211\t              vm (get code seg')]})\n   212\t    (fn? f)\n   213\t    (let [result (apply f args)]\n   214\t      (if (module/effect? result)\n   215\t        (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n   216\t              (engine/handle-effect\n   217\t                (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n   218\t                result\n   219\t                {:park-entry-fns (call-park-entries seg pc E St K)})]\n   220\t          (if blocked?\n   221\t            {:stop (assoc state :control nil :k nil)}\n   222\t            {:goto [seg (inc pc) value St E K state (get code seg)]}))\n   223\t        {:goto [seg (inc pc) result St E K vm (get code seg)]}))\n   224\t    :else (throw (ex-info \"Cannot apply non-function\" {:fn f}))))\n   225\t\n   226\t\n   227\t;; =============================================================================\n   228\t;; The hot loop\n   229\t;; =============================================================================\n   230\t\n   231\t(defn- vm-hot\n   232\t  \"Run loaded work with the registers in loop locals.\n   233\t\n   234\t   `fuel`, when non-nil, is a number of instructions to execute before\n   235\t   materializing: `step` supplies 1 and `run` supplies nil. The loop exits by\n   236\t   returning a record when the machine halts, parks, blocks on an effect, or\n   237\t   runs out of fuel; every non-exit transition is one `case` arm and one\n   238\t   operation on locals (§4.3). A machine with no control and no continuation\n   239\t   is between evaluations, so one scheduler round is run instead.\"\n   240\t  [vm fuel]\n   241\t  (if (and (nil? (:control vm)) (nil? (:k vm)))\n   242\t    (scheduler-round vm)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
