{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1242,1262p;1050,1066p;1900,1944p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1050\t```\n  1051\t\n  1052\tRules:\n  1053\t\n  1054\t- **The child is a task, not a call.** The scheduler holds\n  1055\t  `:installs {name {:phase p :vm child :waiters [task-ids] :parent\n  1056\t  link-id}}`. The child is a VM of the same backend with a fresh,\n  1057\t  isolated store containing nothing of any other task's state (UCF\n  1058\t  section 7.6.2 applied to loading), its own wait-set, and a module\n  1059\t  view of its own: each entry the parent task already holds is\n  1060\t  attached and lowered into the child's coordinates at its creation,\n  1061\t  acts 3 and 4 run for the child as a receiving task, never the\n  1062\t  parent's lowered values reused (r6; the parent's coordinates are\n  1063\t  meaningless in the child's fresh kernel). The scheduler steps it in\n  1064\t  its ordinary round beside every other task; nothing about it runs\n  1065\t  inside wait restoration.\n  1066\t- **The requiring task waits on the install, not on the linker.** Once\n  1242\t\n  1243\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\n  1244\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\n  1245\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of exactly one\n  1246\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\n  1247\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The lift\n  1248\t  finds it by the walker's own row index (`node -> row id`, which\n  1249\t  `vm-load-rows` builds when it decodes rows into nodes and which\n  1250\t  `attach-image` extends), and refuses the lift as\n  1251\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :unrooted-body` when the node\n  1252\t  is in no held row, which can happen only for a closure a host\n  1253\t  function fabricated. The origin image of a walker closure is the\n  1254\t  tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda` row id is\n  1255\t  content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two trees is one id\n  1256\t  and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads the parent's row at\n  1257\t  that id (present after act 3) and decodes its body slot into a node\n  1258\t  through the same decoder the loader uses, so the closure's `:body`\n  1259\t  in the parent is structurally equal to the child's and its `:params`\n  1260\t  and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\n  1261\t\n  1262\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\n  1900\t    walker closure lowers to a body structurally equal to the child's;\n  1901\t    on every backend, a parent parked mid-program at a nonzero pc with\n  1902\t    a non-empty stack, frames, and continuation resumes after\n  1903\t    `attach-image` at the same pc with the same stack, frames, and\n  1904\t    restore identities, and every register the loader would have reset\n  1905\t    is unchanged; the parked entry predates the attach, its recorded\n  1906\t    image is a row of the offset table, no restore writes `:segment`\n  1907\t    from an entry, and `:hash` is never a restore key; a continuation\n  1908\t    lifted after an attach and lowered into a fresh kernel places\n  1909\t    every pc by the receiving offset table; two waiters holding\n  1910\t    images of different lengths each lower the same slice against\n  1911\t    their own coordinates and apply the export correctly.\n  1912\t18. Under `:derivation :verifying`, a manifest whose record names a\n  1913\t    profile the linker does not implement is `:unverified-derivation`,\n  1914\t    never installed; under `:trusted` the same manifest installs with\n  1915\t    `:trust :composition` in the outcome.\n  1916\t19. A request naming a mismatched contract is refused with no content\n  1917\t    request appended and no validator run.\n  1918\t20. A closure re-exported from a transitive dependency lifts with that\n  1919\t    dependency's image as origin, the parent loads that image before\n  1920\t    lowering, and an origin the scheduler never verified is\n  1921\t    `:foreign-image`; a positional marker offered to a named kernel is\n  1922\t    `:binding-mismatch`.\n  1923\t21. An exact duplicate of a valid envelope, appended any number of\n  1924\t    times, is honored exactly once and never marks its principal as\n  1925\t    equivocating; a replayed envelope with an old sequence, an envelope\n  1926\t    at or below a principal's floor, two distinct envelopes with one\n  1927\t    sequence, a retraction naming no assertion, and a retraction by\n  1928\t    another principal are each discarded with the stated kind; a\n  1929\t    retraction bound to an assertion's id removes that assertion only.\n  1930\t22. One four-way manifest links through all four kernels with no\n  1931\t    `:contract-mismatch`; a manifest of another schema version is\n  1932\t    `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:schema`; a derivation without a\n  1933\t    contract entry is `:descriptor-defect` with rule\n  1934\t    `:contract-missing`.\n  1935\t23. On every backend, a module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports a\n  1936\t    closure reading `x` links, and the closure applied in the parent\n  1937\t    reads the module's `x`, while a parent binding of the same name\n  1938\t    neither supplies nor shadows it; a write the closure makes to `x`\n  1939\t    is visible to a sibling export applied afterwards in the same\n  1940\t    task and to no other task's module store; the same module linked\n  1941\t    by two tasks holds two isolated stores; in the child, a closure\n  1942\t    re-exported from a dependency reads that dependency's module\n  1943\t    store, not the child's ambient bindings.\n  1944\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-24 13:07:50 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 20:07:50 ICT\n\nVerdict: REJECTED\n\n1. **CLOSED — Dominance and scanners.** The four format records now use position-bearing scanners, and lambda-body reads retain obligations unless a definition dominates every possible application site. The unsafe “it will error at runtime” assumption is withdrawn. [linker.md:280](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:280), [linker.md:450](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:450), [linker.md:594](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:594)\n\n2. **PARTIAL — Hash-safe attachment and per-task lowering.** Parked-entry restore and per-task lowering are now specified. Ordinary *register return* remains a separate path: its current transition writes a saved `:segment` and `:hash` back into the VM. If a nested `require` attaches an image during a call, returning discards that image despite r6’s grow-only inventory rule. Amend return frames/transitions as well as parked restores, and test attach-during-call followed by return. [linker.md:1128](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1128), [linker.md:1152](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1152), [register.cljc:184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:184)\n\n3. **PARTIAL — Module-local stores.** Carrying the child’s store fixes a module’s own exported closure, but the stated write rule is not covered by the proposed kernel amendment: stack, register, and semantic `:store-put` instructions write directly to ambient `:store`, while r6 says the only kernel behavioral amendment is free-name resolution. Specify module-store routing for direct store instructions and save/restore the active store context across nested calls. Also, a re-exported dependency closure carries `:store-of` for that dependency, yet the portable slice carries only the current child’s halted store; a new receiving task may have no dependency store. Include and verify transitively referenced module-store snapshots, including mutations made in the child. [linker.md:1274](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1274), [linker.md:1295](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1295), [linker.md:1817](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1817), [stack.cljc:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:477)\n\nOne additional relocation edge needs specification: the walker assumes a body node belongs to exactly one `:lambda` row. Shared, content-addressed body rows can be referenced by lambdas with different parameters, so a `node -> row id` lookup alone cannot select the lambda. Include the closure’s parameters or source lambda ID in the lookup. [linker.md:1243](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1243), [vm.cljc:1189](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-universal-linker/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1189)\n\nThe 2,257-line file is pure ASCII with no lines over 80 columns. **Consensus has not been reached; r6 is not ready to commit.**"}}
